WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.827
Hi, I'm Jiggle!

00:00:02.827 --> 00:00:08.041
People are turning ordinary language models into decision models with a single serving call.

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:10.275
Here's what that call actually computes.

00:00:11.892 --> 00:00:15.664
The idea: the readout is a serving feature, not a model feature.

00:00:15.664 --> 00:00:19.122
One prefill, then read the probabilities of the tokens you name.

00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:24.222
You send a prompt and your options' token ids.

00:00:24.222 --> 00:00:27.640
The server runs one prefill with max new tokens at zero.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.281
At the last position, the model has a logit for every vocabulary entry.

00:00:32.701 --> 00:00:36.298
It reads only the rows you named: each one's real probability, so

00:00:36.298 --> 00:00:37.796
they needn't sum to one.

00:00:38.216 --> 00:00:40.925
Turn on softmax and the slice is renormalised.

00:00:40.925 --> 00:00:45.327
That deletes the mass on every other token, which was the model declining.

00:00:46.858 --> 00:00:50.712
Put lettered options in the prompt and read the letters: one

00:00:50.712 --> 00:00:52.113
pass, but order matters.

00:00:52.113 --> 00:00:54.566
Reversing it changed ten of thirty-six answers.

00:00:54.986 --> 00:00:58.688
Put each option in its own prompt and read Yes: a pass

00:00:58.688 --> 00:01:00.848
per option, and none can see another.

00:01:00.848 --> 00:01:02.082
Isolation, with nothing trained.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:05.216
Where the headline speedup comes from.

00:01:05.636 --> 00:01:10.097
The baseline types its answer one token at a time, about fifteen milliseconds each.

00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:14.970
The scored path is one prefill, then every candidate branch in one batched pass.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:19.469
So the ratio is just how many tokens the baseline typed: two

00:01:19.469 --> 00:01:21.509
hundred twenty-six gave about sixty-three times.

00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:27.123
The serving half is free on SGLang; calibration needs labelled outcomes, so

00:01:27.123 --> 00:01:30.452
a published calibration number shows who did the rest.

00:01:32.108 --> 00:01:35.722
To recap: one pass, read at the tokens you name, and

00:01:35.722 --> 00:01:37.037
calibration still costs labels.

00:01:37.457 --> 00:01:39.567
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:40.169
I'm Jiggle.

00:01:40.169 --> 00:01:40.471
Bye!
